explanations.E0063:
# E0063: field or method that does not exist

A field read or a method call must name a member of the receiver's type: a field or method of its class, the array methods `len()`, `sum()` and `contains(x)`, the `string` methods `len()`, `chars()`, `bytes()` and `words()`, or the methods of `f64` and `i64`, such as `x.sqrt()` and `n.max(m)`. A built-in type's name takes a constant: `f64.pi`, `f64.tau`, `f64.e`, `f64.infinity`, `f64.nan`, `f64.max`, `f64.min_positive` and `f64.epsilon`. No other type has any, so `i64.max` is this error too. A method of `f64` is no method of `i64`: `n.sqrt()` with an `i64` `n` is this error too. A constructor's labels must name fields of its class, or of the labeled payload of its variant. The methods of an enum count as methods of its type, but its value has no fields: that is [E0121](E0121.md).

When a member of the receiver's type has a similar name, the error offers it as an unsafe fix. The unsafe fix may be wrong: the name could be a member that was never written. For `let x = 2.5` and `x.sqrtt()`, the unsafe fix is `sqrt`; for `f64.PI`, it is `pi`.

The same error is reported for a method call on a value of a type parameter: the type parameter has only the methods of its bounds. When exactly one interface of the program declares a method of that name, and it takes no type parameters, the help adds the bound: for `x.area()` on a value of type `T`, `add a bound: T: Shape`. If `T` has bounds already, the help lists them with the new one.

## Why

Sisu does not resolve a misspelled member name on its own. A misspelled name would otherwise read some other value or run some other code.

## Broken

```sisu
class Point {
    x: i64
}

fn main() {
    let p = Point(x: 1)
    print(p.y)
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
class Point {
    x: i64
}

fn main() {
    let p = Point(x: 1)
    print(p.x)
}
```

explanations.E0152:
# E0152: conformance that a type does not meet

A class or an enum conforms to an interface when it lists the interface after its name (`class Circle: Shape`), or when an `extend Circle: Shape` block names it. The type then holds every requirement of the interface, as a method with the same signature.

## Why

A conformance is a promise: each function that takes any `Shape` can call `area` on the type. A missing method breaks the promise. A method with another parameter type or another return type breaks it too. The method must have the same parameter types and the same return type as the requirement, with `Self` as the conforming type. It must have the same number of type parameters of its own, with the same bounds. The parameter names can differ.

A requirement can be an associated type. A conformance meets `type Item` with a choice, `type Item = i64`, in the body of the class or in the `extend` block, and the help writes `type Item` for each one it lacks. A choice for a name that no interface of the conformance declares is an error too, such as `type Item = i64` in a block for an interface with no `Item`. The chosen type must meet the bounds that the interface gives its associated type ([E0154](E0154.md)).

In an `extend Circle: Shape` block, every method is a requirement of `Shape`. A method that is not a requirement belongs in `extend Circle { ... }`, or in the body of the class. The body of a class that lists an interface holds the methods of the interface and the methods of the class. A listed interface also needs the requirements of the interfaces it inherits.

## Broken

```sisu
interface Shape {
    fn area(self) -> f64
}

class Circle: Shape {
    r: f64
}

fn main() {
    print(Circle(r: 1.0).r)
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
interface Shape {
    fn area(self) -> f64
}

class Circle: Shape {
    r: f64

    fn area(self) -> f64 {
        3.0 * self.r * self.r
    }
}

fn main() {
    print(Circle(r: 1.0).area())
}
```

## Language interfaces

The prelude has the language interfaces `Display`, `Eq`, `Ord` and `AnyObject`. The compiler writes a conformance to some of them, and a type that writes its own replaces it. A type needs no method when the compiler writes the conformance:

- `Display` is written for every type. A class or an enum that lists `Display` needs no `to_string`. A method with that name in the body of a type, or in an `extend` block with no interface, is the `Display` of the type, and it must have the signature `fn to_string(self) -> string`.
- `Eq` is written for an enum whose payload types all have `Eq`. `enum Expr: Eq { ... }` needs no `eq` method. When a payload type has none, as a class does, the error names it: "`H` gets no generated `Eq`, since `Node` has none". Write the `eq` method, or give the payload type an `Eq`.
- `Eq` is not written for a class, and `Ord` is not written for a class or an enum. `class Node: Eq {}` is this error, with the note that `Node` gets no generated `Eq`. Write the method: `eq` for `Eq`, and `less` for `Ord`. A type that lists `Ord` also needs `Eq`, because `Ord` inherits it.

## Generic interfaces

For an interface with type parameters, the help writes each requirement with the type arguments of the conformance. With `interface Convert<T> { fn convert(self) -> T }` and `extend Meter: Convert<i64> {}`, the requirement is `fn convert(self) -> i64`. The arguments also stand in the bounds of the requirement's own type parameters: for `fn mix<U: Convert<T>>(self, other: U) -> T` and `extend Meter: Mix<i64> {}`, the requirement is `fn mix<U: Convert<i64>>(self, other: U) -> i64`. A type parameter of the requirement takes a new name when an argument or a parameter of the block has its name: for `extend<U> Box<U>: Mix<U> {}` the requirement is `fn mix<U2: Convert<U>>(self, other: U2) -> U`, because `U` in the block is another type.

## Related

- [E0153](E0153.md): a second conformance of a type to an interface.
- [E0154](E0154.md): a conformance that needs another conformance.

explanations.E0153:
# E0153: second conformance to an interface

A type conforms to an interface once. A class that lists the interface after its name already conforms, and so does the type of an `extend Circle: Shape` block. A second conformance to the same interface is an error.

This is also true of an interface that the compiler writes. `class Circle: Display, Display` and `enum Light: Eq` with `extend Light: Eq {}` list the interface twice. Remove one of them.

When the second conformance writes the methods, the first gets no [E0152](E0152.md) for them: remove the listing, or move the methods into the type and then remove the block.

A call of a method that only the second block writes gets no [E0063](E0063.md) beside this error. The compiler does not check the methods of the second block against the interface until you remove one of the two conformances.

## Why

Two conformances of one type to one interface give two answers to a call through the interface, and Sisu has no rule to choose between them. Keep one of them. If the two blocks hold different methods, move the methods that you want into the one you keep.

A generic interface can have several conformances for one type, if their type arguments differ. `extend Celsius: Convert<Fahrenheit>` and `extend Celsius: Convert<Kelvin>` are two conformances. Two blocks with the same type arguments clash, and the error shows them: `Celsius` conforms to `Convert<Kelvin>` twice. The type arguments of the interface count in the same way as the target. `extend<T> Box<T>: Convert<T>` and `extend<U> Box<U>: Convert<i64>` clash on `Box<i64>: Convert<i64>`. `extend<U> Box<U>: Convert<[U]>` does not clash with `extend<T> Box<T>: Convert<T>`, as no `T` can be `[T]`.

Two conformances clash when one type fits both targets. `extend<T> Stack<T>: Named` and `extend Stack<i64>: Named` clash on `Stack<i64>`, and the error names that type. `extend<T> Pair<T, i64>: Named` and `extend<U> Pair<i64, U>: Named` clash on `Pair<i64, i64>`. Two blocks that no type fits together, such as `extend<T> Pair<T, [T]>: Named` and `extend<U> Pair<[U], U>: Named`, do not clash.

## Broken

```sisu
interface Shape {
    fn area(self) -> f64
}

class Circle: Shape {
    r: f64

    fn area(self) -> f64 {
        3.0 * self.r * self.r
    }
}

extend Circle: Shape {
    fn area(self) -> f64 {
        0.0
    }
}

fn main() {
    print(Circle(r: 1.0).area())
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
interface Shape {
    fn area(self) -> f64
}

class Circle: Shape {
    r: f64

    fn area(self) -> f64 {
        3.0 * self.r * self.r
    }
}

fn main() {
    print(Circle(r: 1.0).area())
}
```

## Related

- [E0152](E0152.md): a conformance that a type does not meet.
